Systems Engineering & Cybersecurity Foundations – Team Assignments


📌 Project Overview

This repository contains team-based systems engineering and cybersecurity coursework completed as part of graduate-level study. The work focuses on operational needs analysis, stakeholder analysis, system requirements, and system behavior modeling, applied to real-world problem domains.

The projects demonstrate how complex systems are analyzed, structured, and validated using formal methods such as operational needs, functional decomposition, use-case sequence diagrams (UCSD), context diagrams, external system diagrams (ESD), and requirements specification 


🧩 Project Themes

The assignments span two major applied system domains:

🚗 Smart Campus Parking System (Assignment 1)

A conceptual system designed to minimize parking search time and travel distance on the Rutgers University–New Brunswick campus.

Key focus areas:

Operational needs definition

Stakeholder identification and classification

Voice of the Customer (VoC) interviews

Privacy, security, and safety considerations

Integration with campus systems, navigation, and payment platforms

The system emphasizes real-time data, user preferences, fairness, privacy controls, and secure integrations 

📱 Smartphone System Modeling & Requirements (Assignment 2)

A detailed system-level analysis of a smartphone, modeled using formal systems engineering techniques.

Key focus areas:

Functional decomposition (Fn-1 to Fn-6)

Authentication and access control

Network services (cellular, GPS, Wi-Fi)

Maintenance and hostile condition response

System-level input and output requirements

New use cases such as Emergency Services (SOS) and Take a Picture

Artifacts include use-case sequence diagrams, context diagrams, IDEF0 models, and expanded requirements, showing how a secure system responds to both normal and hostile conditions 


🎯 Learning Objectives

Apply systems thinking to real-world problems

Translate stakeholder needs into structured system requirements

Model system behavior using industry-standard diagrams

Understand security, privacy, availability, and reliability at the system level

Bridge cybersecurity concepts with system design and operations

🛠️ Methods & Techniques Used

Operational Needs Analysis

Stakeholder Mapping (Active vs Passive)

Voice of the Customer (VoC) Interviews

Functional Decomposition

Use Case Sequence Diagrams (UCSD)

Context Diagrams (CD)

External Systems Diagrams (ESD)

Formal “shall”-based requirements specification

🛡️ Cybersecurity Relevance

These projects directly support cybersecurity learning through:

Authentication and authorization modeling

Privilege separation (user vs provider vs admin)

Privacy-aware system design

Secure interaction with external systems

Availability, resilience, and hostile condition response

The work demonstrates how security must be embedded at the requirements and design stage, not added later.

